          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              Il s’agit là d’une question qu’un arithméticien ne peut guère manquer de se poser; on n’aperçoit d’ailleurs aucun motif sérieux de parier pour ou contre. Peut-être dira-t-on que l’existence d’une infinité de solutions rationnelles pour une équation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x,y)=0
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Weil79"} Commentaire 1979
          %p
            The
            %em Mordell conjecture
            or
            %em Falting’s theorem
            is a statement about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finiteness
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+points"} rational points
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+curve"} algebraic curve
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+field"} number field
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \gt 1
          %p
            Its statement motivated the introduction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anabelian+geometry"} anabelian geometry
            (
            %a{:href => "#Grothendieck"}> Grothendieck
            ).
          %p
            The Mordell conjecture completed a classification of the behavior of rational points on curves over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^1
            and hence infinitely many. For genus 1, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordell-Weil+theorem"} Mordell-Weil theorem
            tells us it is either empty or a finitely generated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            (sometimes finite, sometimes infinite). To finish off this type of classification the Mordell conjecture shows that only the finite case can occur for higher genus.
          %p
            This result also implies many non-trivial results. For example, fix a finite set of primes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %span.newWikiWord
              polarization degree
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/polarization+degree"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+varieties"} abelian varieties
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and polarization degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            with
            %span.newWikiWord
              bad reduction
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/bad+reduction"} ?
            inside
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K3"} K3
          %h2#sketch_of_faltings_proof Sketch of Faltings’ proof
          %p
            We follow the seminar notes
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BhattSnowden"} BhattSnowden
          %p The proof proceeds along the following steps:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Using
                %em Parshin’s trick
                and passing to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobian+variety"} Jacobian variety
                to reduce the Mordell conjecture to the
                %em Shafarevich conjecture
                that there are only finitely many
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+varieties"} abelian varieties
                of dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                and polarization degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                with good reduction outside a fixed set of places
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                Using the semisimplicity of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tate+module"} Tate module
                and the
                %em isogeny theorem
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mi ℓ
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ℓ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi ℓ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{K}(A,B)\otimes\mathbb{Z}_{\ell}\cong Hom_{G_{K}}(T_{\ell}(A),T_{\ell}(B))
              %p
                for abelian varieties
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %li
              %p
                Showing that the semisimplicity of the Tate module and the isogeny theorem will follow if there are only finitely many abelian varieties (up to isomorphism) isogenous to a fixed abelian variety
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                (follows a strategy originally used by Tate to prove the analogous result over finite fields).
            %li
              %p
                Showing that there are only finitely many abelian varieties (up to isomorphism) isogenous to a fixed abelian variety
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                by relating the Faltings height to the moduli-theoretic height (for which there are only finitely many points of bounded height) using
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arakelov+theory"} Arakelov theory
                then studying how the Faltings height changes under isogeny. This makes use of the Hodge-Tate decomposition from
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+Hodge+theory"} p-adic Hodge theory
          %h2#other_proofs Other proofs
          %p
            Other proofs of the Mordell conjecture using different methods have been given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Vojta"} Paul Vojta
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vojta91"}> Vojta91
            ) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Bombieri"} Enrico Bombieri
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bombieri90"}> Bombieri90
            ). Recently another proof involving period mappings together with o-minimality methods was given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Lawrence"} Brian Lawrence
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Akshay+Venkatesh"} Akshay Venkatesh
            (
            %a{:href => "#LawrenceVenkatesh18"}> LawrenceVenkatesh18
            ).
          %h2#effective_mordell_conjecture Effective Mordell conjecture
          %p
            Faltings’ proof of the Mordell conjecture determines the finiteness of the rational points but it does not find the set of rational points. The latter problem is also known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em effective Mordell conjecture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minhyong+Kim"} Minhyong Kim
            and is related to his proposed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+gauge+theory"} arithmetic gauge theory
            (see also
            %a{:href => "{#Balakrishnan19}"} Balakrishnan19
            for a survey).
          %h2#relation_to_other_statements Relation to other statements
          %p
            The Mordell conjecture is implied by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abc+conjecture"} abc conjecture
          %p
            The Mordell conjecture implies
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Tate's isogeny theorem
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tate%27s+isogeny+theorem"} ?
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vojta%27s+conjecture"} Vojta's conjecture
          %h2#references References
          %p The Mordell conjecture originates in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Mordell"}> Louis Mordell
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the rational solutions of the indeterminate equation of the third and fourth degrees
          %p It was proven in
          %ul
            %li#Faltings83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerd+Faltings"}> Gerd Faltings
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Endlichkeitssätze für abelsche Varietäten über Zahlkörpern
              %strong 73
              3 (1983) 349-366 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01388432"}> doi:10.1007/BF01388432
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Seminar notes on Faltings’ proof:
          %ul
            %li#BhattSnowden
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"}> Bhargav Bhatt
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Snowden"} Andrew Snowden
              (org.):
              %em Faltings’ proof of the Mordell conjecture
              (seminar notes, 2016) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://web.math.princeton.edu/~takumim/Mordell.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BhattSnowden-FaltingsProofofMordell.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p Other proofs after Faltings:
          %ul
            %li#Vojta91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Vojta"}> Paul Vojta
                , 1991,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Siegel’s theorem in the compact case
            %li#Bombieri90
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Bombieri"}> Enrico Bombieri
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Mordell conjecture revisited
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Annali della Scuola Normale Superiore di Pisa - Classe di Scienze
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=ASNSP_1990_4_17_4_615_0"} numdam:ASNSP_1990_4_17_4_615_0
            %li#LawrenceVenkatesh18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Lawrence"}> Brian Lawrence
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Akshay+Venkatesh"} Akshay Venkatesh
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Diophantine problems and
                    = succeed "-adic" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi p
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                %strong 221
                (2020) 893–999 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02721"}> arxiv:1807.02721
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-020-00966-7"} doi:10.1007/s00222-020-00966-7
          %p Reviews:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spencer+Bloch"}> Spencer Bloch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Proof of the Mordell Conjecture
                %strong 6
                2 (1984) 41-47 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/BF03024155.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF03024155"} doi:10.1007/BF03024155
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barry+Mazur"}> Barry Mazur
                ,
                %em Abelian varieties and the Mordell-Lang conjecture
                (
                %a{:href => "http://library.msri.org/books/Book39/files/mazur.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p A survey of two approaches to the effective Mordell conjecture:
          %ul
            %li#Balakrishnan19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jennifer+Balakrishnan"}> Jennifer Balakrishnan
              , A. J. Best, F. Bianchi,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Lawrence"} Brian Lawrence
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Two recent
                  = succeed "-adic" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %em Arithmetic L-Functions and Differential Geometric Methods
              Progress in Mathematics
              %strong 338
              Birkhäuser (2021) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12755"}> arxiv:1910.12755
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-65203-6_2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-65203-6_2
          %p Encyclopedia entries:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Encyclopedia+of+Mathematics"}> Encyclopedia of Mathematics
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Mordell_conjecture"} Mordell conjecture
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordell_conjecture#CITEREFMordell1922"} Faltings’ theorem
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anabelian+geometry"} anabelian geometry
            originates in
          %ul#Grothendieck
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandre+Grothendieck"}> Alexandre Grothendieck
              , letter to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faltings"} Faltings
              (1983) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~leila/grothendieckcircle/GtoF.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The Weil quote stems from
          %ul
            %li#Weil79
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"}> André Weil
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Œuvres Scientifiques - Collected Papers Volume III
